1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,069
Lesson 1.1.

2
00:00:02,449 --> 00:00:05,100
Laws of thermodynamics and free energy.

3
00:00:06,217 --> 00:00:09,140
A chip is a heat engine
running in reverse: it

4
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:12,190
receives electrical work
and turns it into heat.

5
00:00:12,570 --> 00:00:16,724
If we want to compute with
physics, and not in spite of it, we

6
00:00:16,724 --> 00:00:20,275
need the rules that govern
energy, heat and disorder.

7
00:00:20,655 --> 00:00:24,798
In this lesson we review the laws
of thermodynamics and introduce

8
00:00:24,798 --> 00:00:28,431
the quantity we will use most
in the course: free energy.

9
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:33,931
Let's put two boxes of particles
in contact, one hot and one cold.

10
00:00:34,311 --> 00:00:39,063
In each collision across the wall, two
particles share their energy at random.

11
00:00:39,443 --> 00:00:42,883
Nobody tells the energy
where to go, and yet the mean

12
00:00:42,883 --> 00:00:46,258
energy of the two boxes
converges to the same value.

13
00:00:46,638 --> 00:00:50,912
That final state, with no net
flow, is thermal equilibrium, and

14
00:00:50,912 --> 00:00:54,507
temperature is precisely
the quantity that equalizes.

15
00:00:54,887 --> 00:00:59,295
The arrow of time emerges on its own,
from collisions that are symmetric.

16
00:01:00,417 --> 00:01:02,995
The zeroth law says thermal equilibrium is

17
00:01:02,995 --> 00:01:05,941
transitive, and that lets
us define temperature.

18
00:01:06,321 --> 00:01:10,691
The first law is energy conservation:
the change in internal energy

19
00:01:10,691 --> 00:01:14,410
is the heat that enters plus
the work done on the system.

20
00:01:14,790 --> 00:01:19,018
The second law says the total
entropy of an isolated system never

21
00:01:19,018 --> 00:01:23,442
decreases; for a system in contact
with a bath at temperature T, the

22
00:01:23,442 --> 00:01:27,215
entropy change is at least the
heat received divided by T.

23
00:01:27,595 --> 00:01:30,670
The third law sets the
zero: entropy tends to

24
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:33,336
a minimum as temperature tends to zero.

25
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:35,491
What is entropy?

26
00:01:35,871 --> 00:01:40,494
Boltzmann answered: S = k_B
ln Ω, where Ω is the number

27
00:01:40,494 --> 00:01:44,445
of microstates compatible
with what we observe.

28
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:46,043
Think of a hundred coins.

29
00:01:46,423 --> 00:01:49,440
The macrostate fifty heads
can be achieved in vastly

30
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:52,109
more ways than the
macrostate a hundred heads.

31
00:01:52,489 --> 00:01:56,481
As the number of coins grows,
the distribution concentrates

32
00:01:56,481 --> 00:02:00,676
around one half: its relative
width decays as one over root N.

33
00:02:01,056 --> 00:02:05,441
The second law is statistical: the
system ends up in the macrostates with

34
00:02:05,441 --> 00:02:09,645
more microstates simply because they
are overwhelmingly more probable.

35
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:15,845
At fixed temperature, the system neither
minimizes its energy nor maximizes

36
00:02:15,845 --> 00:02:20,329
its entropy separately: it minimizes
the free energy, F = U − T S.

37
00:02:20,709 --> 00:02:21,866
It is a competition.

38
00:02:22,246 --> 00:02:26,674
Consider two states: one with low
energy and a single microstate,

39
00:02:26,674 --> 00:02:30,761
and another with energy ε higher
but a thousand microstates.

40
00:02:31,141 --> 00:02:35,137
At low temperature energy wins and
the system prefers the low state.

41
00:02:35,517 --> 00:02:39,860
As temperature rises, the entropy
term, T S, grows until it dominates,

42
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:43,582
and the population flips to the
state with many microstates.

43
00:02:43,962 --> 00:02:47,183
The transition happens when
the free energies are equal.

44
00:02:48,283 --> 00:02:50,969
Free energy has an operational meaning.

45
00:02:51,349 --> 00:02:57,479
In a constant-temperature process,
the work needed to take a system from

46
00:02:57,479 --> 00:03:03,182
one state to another is at least
the change in free energy: W ≥ ΔF.

47
00:03:03,562 --> 00:03:07,212
Equality is reached only with
infinitely slow processes.

48
00:03:07,592 --> 00:03:11,722
The difference, W minus ΔF,
is work dissipated as heat,

49
00:03:11,722 --> 00:03:15,250
and it is always greater
than or equal to zero.

50
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:20,825
This inequality is what, in lesson
1.5, gives the cost of erasing a bit.

51
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:24,227
Let's put numbers into
the two-state system.

52
00:03:24,607 --> 00:03:29,095
With an energy gap of 0.1 electronvolts
and a thousand microstates,

53
00:03:29,095 --> 00:03:33,448
the transition temperature is the
energy divided by k_B times the

54
00:03:33,448 --> 00:03:37,199
log of a thousand: about one
hundred sixty-eight kelvin.

55
00:03:37,579 --> 00:03:42,003
At three hundred kelvin the free-energy
difference is negative, about

56
00:03:42,003 --> 00:03:46,428
minus seventy-nine millielectronvolts,
and the fraction of the system

57
00:03:46,428 --> 00:03:50,147
in the many-microstate state
is about ninety-five percent.

58
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:53,996
In a computer these
laws are very concrete.

59
00:03:54,376 --> 00:03:58,898
By the first law, all electrical power
ends up as heat that must be removed.

60
00:03:59,278 --> 00:04:02,787
By the second, erasing information
requires expelling entropy.

61
00:04:03,167 --> 00:04:07,705
And in a thermodynamic chip, thermal
noise drives the system toward low

62
00:04:07,705 --> 00:04:11,540
free-energy states: that is the
basis of Boltzmann sampling.

63
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:16,183
In the next lesson we will go
from counting microstates to

64
00:04:16,183 --> 00:04:19,776
the Boltzmann distribution,
and see that the free energy is

65
00:04:19,776 --> 00:04:22,943
minus k_B T times the log
of the partition function.

66
00:04:23,323 --> 00:04:27,001
In unit three we will refine
the work inequality with the

67
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:30,615
Jarzynski equality, which
holds even for fast processes.

68
00:04:31,717 --> 00:04:36,795
Beware of some confusions: heat is
transferred energy, not temperature.

69
00:04:37,175 --> 00:04:40,328
A system's entropy can
decrease, as long as the

70
00:04:40,328 --> 00:04:43,079
environment's increases at least as much.

71
00:04:43,459 --> 00:04:46,531
Saying entropy is disorder
is only a metaphor.

72
00:04:46,911 --> 00:04:49,707
And pay attention to the
sign convention for work.

73
00:04:50,817 --> 00:04:53,883
In summary: equilibrium
is the state with no net

74
00:04:53,883 --> 00:04:56,439
flows, and temperature characterizes it.

75
00:04:56,819 --> 00:05:00,363
Energy is conserved and total
entropy does not decrease.

76
00:05:00,742 --> 00:05:06,206
Entropy counts microstates, and at
fixed temperature the system minimizes

77
00:05:06,206 --> 00:05:11,146
the free energy, F = U − T S, which
also bounds the required work.

78
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:16,601
To practice: count the microstates
of fifty and of a hundred heads.

79
00:05:16,981 --> 00:05:20,556
Compute the population of the
two-state system at a hundred kelvin.

80
00:05:20,936 --> 00:05:23,935
Apply the first law to
a heat and work balance.

81
00:05:24,315 --> 00:05:27,171
And explain why equality
between work and free

82
00:05:27,171 --> 00:05:29,841
energy requires an
infinitely slow process.
